  "live_records": {
    "q-121513": {
      "dom_id": "step-q121513",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are written in ascending order,  what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{3}?$",
      "worked_solution": "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x}{2} & ={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & \\frac{3x}{2} & ={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our two equations for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & ={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 3x & ={480}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘} \\\\ x & =\\frac{{240}^{∘}}{3}+\\frac{n\\cdot {720}^{∘}}{3} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{{480}^{∘}}{3}+\\frac{n\\cdot {720}^{∘}}{3} \\\\ x & ={80}^{∘}+n\\cdot {240}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={160}^{∘}+n\\cdot {240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={80}^{∘}$ and $x={160}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={320}^{∘}$ and $x={400}^{∘}.$ We reject the solution ${400}^{∘}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx={80}^{∘},\\,{160}^{∘},\\,{320}^{∘}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{1}={80}^{∘}$ and ${x}_{3}={320}^{∘},$ the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\text{ }+{x}_{3}$ is $80+320=400.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
