    "q-121537": {
      "dom_id": "step-q121537",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Consider the equation $2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2})+3=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{5}$ are written in ascending order, what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2})+3 & =0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}) & =-3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}) & =-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x}{2} & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & \\frac{5x}{2} & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}+4n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 5x & =\\frac{7\\pi }{3}+4n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{4n\\pi }{5} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{7\\pi }{15}+\\frac{4n\\pi }{5} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(12n+5)\\pi }{15} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{(12n+7)\\pi }{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{15}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{17\\pi }{15}$  and $x=\\frac{19\\pi }{15}.$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{29\\pi }{15}$  and $x=\\frac{31\\pi }{15}.$ We reject the solution $\\frac{31\\pi }{15}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4},{x}_{5}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{15},\\,\\frac{17\\pi }{15},\\,\\frac{19\\pi }{15},\\,\\frac{29\\pi }{15}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{17\\pi }{15}=\\frac{17{\\pi }^{2}}{45}.\n$$",
