    "q-8416": {
      "dom_id": "step-q8416",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, what is the value of ${x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}?$",
      "worked_solution": "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & 2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 2x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 2x & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{(1+12n)\\pi }{12} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{(3+4n)\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{13\\pi }{12}$  and $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{13\\pi }{12},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{13\\pi }{12}=\\frac{13{\\pi }^{2}}{16}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
