    "q-338812": {
      "dom_id": "step-q338812",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation $4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})+2=0,$ for $0<x<\\pi ,$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,{x}_{3}=$ \n{{field-3}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})+2 & =0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =-2 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+n\\cdot 2\\pi , & \\, & \\, & 3x-\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+n\\cdot 2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\cdot 2\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 3x & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+n\\cdot 2\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 3x & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+n\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{18}+n\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0<x<\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{18}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{17\\pi }{18}$ and $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$  We reject the solution $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0<x<\\pi .$ \n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{5\\pi }{18},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,{x}_{3}=\\frac{17\\pi }{18}.\n$$",
